#>wonder_brace_semantics_curly_name
?["Does { } name a definition, a direction or a behavior, read as one word everywhere it is taught?"]{
  #:depth #!semantic ~#lens(comparison)
  ~#with: @plan @operator_detection
  ~#taste: "A pair a reader learns once should mean one thing on every page that teaches it."
  ~#hypothesis: "Definition is the word a newcomer brings, and the workbench canon and spw.quest already use it; direction and behavior read as what a definition does."
  ~#tension: "Direction is in the registry and the CSS (--op-direction-color), so the site's paint and its words move together or not at all."
  ~#expect: "The registry says direction and never definition; the conventions say behavior and direction, and definition only where they quote the workbench."
  ~#unblocks: "decisions 2 to 5, which assume a name for the pair, and the < & > instrument"
  ~#probe(run)
  !probe{ "rg -o -w 'definition|direction|behavior' public/js/kernel/operator-detection.js .spw/conventions/semantic-braces.spw .spw/conventions/operator-semantics.spw | sort | uniq -c" }
  $%[curly.definition, curly.direction, curly.behavior]
}

#>wonder_brace_semantics_highlighter_then_now
?["Were the early readings of Spw better than what replaced them, or did the language drift from them: one highlighter color per container across every word of a paragraph, \"of\" read as a concept in {chronicles} <of> (narnia), and <&> as a placeholder for a concept with a subject?"]{
  #:depth #!semantic ~#lens(comparison)
  ~#with: @plan @operator_detection @build_expression_manifest
  ~#taste: "A reading worth keeping lets two people see where their understandings part; the creator raised these ideas again on 2026-11-05 and is not sure they were worse than what usurped them."
  ~#then: "Every word in a passage takes the color of the container it sits in; a preposition can be a concept; <&> is a typed hole, a concept that has a subject."
  ~#now: "< is the concept boundary (operator-detection.js); & is a join between two whole lines, one the subject of the other (compound-expressions.spw#declared_bond_versus_inferred_kinship); * is the only placeholder the shelf search knows (spw.quest /specimens); expressions sit on hosts as handles, not on every word. < & > survives as this plan's instrument (section D)."
  ~#hypothesis: "The two serve different readers. The highlighter reads a passage, a performance of one person's understanding; the handle reads a host, a component's role. The question spw.quest's guide says Spw keeps asking (where do two understandings stop sharing ground) needs the first, and the site's runtime grew around the second."
  ~#tension: "Highlighting every word is subjective, which makes it a performance rather than a fact; searching by the topography a performance implies only works if every reading is kept, with who made it."
  ~#unblocks: "spw-quest slices P and Q; the per-container highlighter paint held on 2026-11-05"
  ~#expect: "A little over the 1,075 hosts section B counted on 2026-10-13: the handle reading keeps growing one host at a time, and no route paints a passage word by word."
  !probe{ "rg -c 'data-spw-semantic-expression' --glob '*.html' . | awk -F: '{s+=$2} END {print s}'" }
  $%[reading.highlighter, reading.handle, reading.both]
}

#>wonder_brace_semantics_first_way_back
?["Which host reads a line back into its controls first: the lens switch that already writes its seat, the < & > instrument, or the cauldron chip?"]{
  #:depth #!pragmatics ~#lens(choice)
  ~#with: @plan @spw_workbench_parser
  ~#taste: "The way back is best learned on a control a reader already uses, so the first lesson is that the line is the control, not that a new widget exists."
  ~#hypothesis: "The lens switch: writeLensModeState in lens-modes.js rewrites its [seat] on every press, and requestLensMode is its one way in, so reading [mode] with readExpressionSlots closes the loop without a second writer, and a node test can hold it before any look is approved."
  ~#tension: "The creator named < & > the first authored element; proving the round trip on another host first could make the instrument a second implementation instead of the pilot. The cauldron chip writes the richest line (*cauldron{…}), but reading it back means setting a cauldron's contents from text."
  ~#expect: "Each of these hosts writes its own expression and none reads one back: writes in lens-modes.js, experiments.js and boonhonk-mixer.js, the chip's line through live-expression.js, and no readExpressionSlots call in any of them."
  ~#unblocks: "Slice 3.5's fixture, and whether Slice 7 is the first host of the contract or the second"
  ~#probe(run)
  !probe{ "rg -n 'spwSemanticExpression =|readExpressionSlots' public/js/runtime/page/lens-modes.js public/js/interface/cauldron/live-expression.js public/js/modules/design/experiments.js public/js/modules/widgets/boonhonk-mixer.js" }
  $%[way_back.lens_switch, way_back.instrument, way_back.cauldron_chip]
}
